18:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-muted/30 border border-border/40 rounded-3xl p-10 md:p-16","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-4 text-3xl font-display font-bold mb-10","children":[["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-book-open text-primary w-10 h-10","children":[["$","path","1akyts",{"d":"M12 7v14"}],["$","path","ruj8y",{"d":"M3 18a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1h5a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 4 4 0 0 1 4-4h5a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1h-6a3 3 0 0 0-3 3 3 3 0 0 0-3-3z"}],"$undefined"]}],"Deep Understanding"]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert max-w-none text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-6","children":"If Buddhi is the executive decision-maker, Manas is the chaotic, overflowing inbox. It coordinates the five senses. Its default state is restless motion. It creates hundreds of thoughts a minute fueled by likes (Raga) and dislikes (Dvesha). Left unsupervised, Manas is an absolute tyrant, constantly whispering anxiety and conjuring worst-case scenarios. Arjuna famously complains to Krishna in the Gita that controlling the Manas is harder than controlling the wind. The entire discipline of early Yoga is devoted to reining in this frantic, terrified, desiring animal."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"italic border-l-2 border-primary/20 pl-6 py-2","children":"The central challenge in classical Yoga and Vedanta. The texts consistently teach that the mind itself is a subtle material object. Therefore, it can be trained, disciplined, and brought to heel like a bicep or a wild horse."}]]}]]}]
